    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Adjusting a treadmill belt means one of two things, and they use the same two bolts:
          changing its tension, so it grips the rollers without slipping, or changing its tracking,
          so it runs down the middle of the deck. Turn both bolts equally and you change tension.
          Turn one and you change tracking. Almost every belt that ends up worse after an
          adjustment got that way because someone did one while trying to do the other.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Adjust a Treadmill Belt: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            To tighten a treadmill belt, unplug the machine, find the two adjustment bolts at the
            rear corners where the rear roller sits, and turn <strong>both</strong> a quarter turn
            clockwise with the hex key supplied with the machine — exactly the same amount each
            side. Plug in, run it at about 3 mph, and plant your weight on the belt. If it still
            hesitates, repeat another quarter turn each side. Stop as soon as the slipping stops,
            then check you can still lift the belt two to three inches off the deck at the centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            To straighten a belt that runs to one side, run it empty at about 3 mph, turn the rear
            bolt on the side it is drifting toward a quarter turn clockwise, and then wait thirty
            seconds before doing anything else. Repeat until it settles.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is the whole job in two paragraphs. The rest of this page is about the order to
            do it in, the check that should come before any tightening, and the ways people turn a
            five-minute adjustment into a damaged belt. If you only want the tension specification,
            it is set out fully in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>;
            if the belt is wandering and the tension is already right, go straight to
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tension-vs-tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Tension Versus Tracking</h2>
<p>
            Both adjustments work by moving the rear roller. Each end of that roller is held by a
            bolt that runs fore and aft through the frame; turning a bolt clockwise draws its end
            of the roller backward, away from the front roller, and turning it anticlockwise lets
            it come forward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tension</strong> is how hard the belt is stretched between the two rollers.
            Move both ends of the rear roller back by the same amount and the belt gets tighter
            while the roller stays square to the frame. Tension is what lets the front roller —
            the one driven by the motor — grip the belt and drag it across the deck under your
            weight. Too little and the belt slips; too much and it loads the bearings, stretches
            the belt and adds drag the motor fights every session.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tracking</strong> is where the belt runs side to side. Move only one end of
            the rear roller and the roller sits very slightly out of square, which steers the belt
            continuously toward one side, the way a conveyor is steered. Tracking adjustments are
            therefore single-bolt adjustments, and they barely change overall tension.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequences are simple and worth fixing in your head before you pick up
            the key. Tension is always set first, because a slack belt will not hold a tracking
            adjustment and a tension change disturbs tracking slightly anyway. Tension changes
            are always equal on both sides. Tracking changes are always one side at a time. And
            every tension adjustment is followed by a tracking check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="do-you-need-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. First, Decide Whether It Needs Adjusting at All</h2>
<p>
            The commonest reason people search for how to tighten a treadmill belt is that the
            belt hesitates or stutters when they plant a foot. That sensation has two causes, and
            only one of them is a loose belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other — in our experience the more common one — is a dry deck. As the lubricant
            between the belt and the deck wears away, friction rises until the front roller can
            no longer reliably drag the belt across the deck under load. It feels exactly like
            slack. Tightening does make the symptom go away, because a tighter belt gives the
            roller more grip, but it does so by adding a tension load on top of a friction load,
            and the motor and controller board pay for both from then on.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the first check is not a bolt. Unplug the machine, lift the belt edge at the middle
            of the deck and run your fingers across the deck surface underneath. Slick, slightly
            waxy-feeling, is fine. Dry, grippy or dusty means it needs lubricant, and you should
            lubricate, run the machine for a few minutes to spread it, and test again before
            considering tension. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>
            covers the method, and
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate</a>
            covers the interval.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second check is the lift test, described below. If the belt already lifts only two
            to three inches at the centre and is still slipping after lubrication, tension is not
            the fault, and tightening further will not fix it — that pattern points to a stretched
            belt, a glazed roller or a drive problem, all covered later on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Tools and Preparation</h2>
<p>
            You need very little, which is part of why this job is so often done badly — it looks
            too simple to need preparation.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The hex key (Allen key) that came with the machine.</strong> It is usually
            stored in a clip under the console or left in the box of assembly parts. If it has gone
            missing, a standard metric and imperial Allen key set will include the size; try keys
            in the bolt head until one seats fully without play.</li>
            <li><strong>A pen and paper, or your phone.</strong> To record how many quarter turns
            you make on each side. This sounds fussy and is the single most useful habit in the
            job.</li>
            <li><strong>A spirit level</strong>, if you have one, for checking the frame before
            any tracking work.</li>
            <li><strong>A torch</strong>, because the bolt heads are often recessed into dark end
            caps.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Put the treadmill on its lowest incline setting before you start, since some machines
            change belt geometry slightly at incline. Clear anything from under and around the
            belt. Then unplug it at the wall, not at the console switch, and take out the safety
            key.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your manual's maintenance section before you begin. A few manufacturers specify
            a particular gap at the centre of the deck, or a torque value for the rear bolts, or an
            adjustment procedure that differs from the general one here. A machine-specific
            instruction always beats a general rule, and following it also keeps you on the right
            side of the warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="find-the-bolts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Finding the Rear Roller Bolts</h2>
<p>
            Go to the back of the machine and look at the two rear corners, where the plastic end
            caps cover the ends of the rear roller. On most home treadmills you will find one bolt
            head on each side, facing backward, either recessed into a hole in the end cap or
            visible on the end of the frame rail. Those are the rear roller adjustment bolts.
          </p>
          <p>
            On some machines the bolts are on the side of the frame rather than the rear face, and
            on a few the end caps have to be unclipped to reach them. Do not confuse them with the
            bolts holding the end caps or the rear feet on; the adjusters are the ones in line with
            the roller axle, and when you turn one you can usually see the roller end move very
            slightly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before turning anything, look at how far each bolt is threaded in. If one side is
            obviously wound in much further than the other, someone has adjusted unequally in the
            past, which is a frequent cause of drift. If both are wound in close to the end of
            their travel, the belt has probably stretched beyond what the machine can take up,
            and adjustment is not the fix — see the section on stretched belts below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lift-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Lift Test: Measure Before and After</h2>
<p>
            With the machine unplugged, stand at the side level with the middle of the deck. Get
            your fingers under the belt edge and lift straight up, firmly but without hauling on
            it. You are measuring the gap that opens between the underside of the belt and the deck
            at that point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two to three inches is correct</strong> on virtually every home treadmill.
            Under two inches is over-tight. Over three to four inches is slack, and worth
            tightening only if the belt is also slipping. Take the reading at the centre of the
            deck's length, because the belt is naturally tighter near the rollers, and take it on
            both sides — a noticeably different lift left and right means the rear roller is not
            square, which is a tracking issue rather than a tension one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Write the number down. It is your starting point, and checking it again after each
            round of tightening is what stops you going past correct. There is nothing underfoot
            that tells you a belt is now too tight; the lift test is the only upper limit you have.
            The full set of checks, including what a failure on each one means, is in our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tighten-step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Tighten a Treadmill Belt, Step by Step</h2>
<p>
            With the deck confirmed lubricated, the belt still slipping, and a lift reading of more
            than about three inches, the tightening itself takes a few minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Unplug and remove the safety key.</strong> Every turn of a bolt happens with
            the machine off. You will plug it back in to test, and unplug it again before the next
            adjustment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Turn the left bolt a quarter turn clockwise.</strong> Seat the hex key fully
            in the bolt head so it does not round off, and turn a quarter of a revolution — the key
            moving from pointing up to pointing sideways, say.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Turn the right bolt exactly the same amount.</strong> Equal turns change
            tension without steering the belt. Unequal turns steer it, which gives you a tracking
            problem on top of the tension problem you started with. Note down "1/4 left, 1/4
            right".
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Plug in and test at about 3 mph.</strong> Stand on the side rails, start the
            belt, step on and walk, then plant your weight firmly on one foot. A correctly
            tensioned belt carries on at the same speed with no hesitation, no give, and no
            sensation of catching up a moment later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. If it still slips, unplug and repeat.</strong> Another quarter turn each
            side, equally. Small increments are the whole technique: a full turn is a large change
            on most machines, and creeping up on correct is far easier than unwinding an
            over-tightened belt back to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>6. Stop the moment the slipping stops, then do the lift test.</strong> If you
            can still raise the belt two inches or more at the centre, you are done with tension.
            If you cannot, you have gone past correct: back both bolts off equally, a quarter turn
            at a time, until you can.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>7. Check tracking.</strong> Any tension change disturbs tracking slightly. Run
            the belt empty for a minute and watch whether it stays put. If it does not, move on to
            the next section.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a rule of thumb, a belt that is merely a little slack usually responds within one to
            three rounds of quarter turns. If you have made four or five rounds without the
            slipping stopping, stop turning: something other than tension is the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adjust-tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Adjusting Tracking After Tensioning</h2>
<p>
            Tracking is adjusted with the belt moving, which makes it the one part of this job
            done with the machine plugged in. Work from the side or the rear of the machine,
            never standing on the belt, and keep the safety key where you can pull it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Run the belt empty at about 3 mph</strong> and watch it for fifteen seconds.
            You want to identify which side it is moving toward — not which side it is
            currently nearer, which may be where it has already ended up, but the direction it is
            still travelling. Running it empty matters, because a user's footstrike can mask or
            create drift.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Turn the rear bolt on that side a quarter turn clockwise.</strong> If the belt
            is running toward the right, turn the right bolt. Turning it clockwise pulls that end
            of the roller back, which steers the belt away from that side. One bolt only — turning
            both together is a tension change, not a tracking change.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Then wait thirty seconds without touching anything.</strong> A quarter turn
            changes the direction the belt will travel, not its position, and at 3 mph the belt
            takes somewhere around twenty to forty seconds to migrate to its new line. Turning again
            during that window stacks adjustments on a belt still responding to the first, and it
            ends up against the opposite rail.
          </p>
          <p>
            If it is still moving toward the same side after thirty seconds, give the same bolt
            another quarter turn clockwise and wait again. If it has started moving the other way,
            you have gone slightly too far: a quarter turn anticlockwise on the same bolt, and
            wait. When it settles with roughly equal margin either side and is no longer
            travelling, stop. Run it for two or three minutes to confirm it holds, then walk on it
            slowly for a minute and check again, because some belts track differently under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is an equivalent alternative: turn the bolt on the opposite side
            anticlockwise by the same amount. It steers the belt the same way but slightly reduces
            overall tension rather than increasing it, which is the better choice if your lift test
            put you at the tight end of the range. Either way, re-check the lift afterwards. The
            reasons behind each of these steps, and what to do when a belt will not stay centred,
            are covered in depth in
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our belt centring guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-tightening-is-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. When Tightening Is the Wrong Fix</h2>
<p>
            Tightening fixes one specific fault — a belt with too little tension to be gripped by
            the front roller. Several other faults produce similar symptoms, and tightening each
            of them either does nothing or makes things worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Friction from a dry or dirty deck.</strong> Covered above and worth repeating,
            because it is the most common misdiagnosis. Lubricate first. If grit and lint have
            built up under the belt, clean it out before lubricating; our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning under a treadmill belt</a>
            covers the method.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A stretched belt.</strong> A belt that needs re-tightening every few weeks, each
            time going slack sooner, is being permanently deformed, and every extra turn
            accelerates it. So is a belt that slips even though it lifts less than two inches.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drive belt slip.</strong> If the running belt pauses at start-up with a brief
            squeal from under the motor hood, the slip may be in the small drive belt between the
            motor and the front roller. The rear bolts have no effect on that; see
            <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">turns on but the belt will not move</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A motor or controller that cannot hold speed.</strong> If the belt slows
            smoothly under your weight rather than slipping — the speed sags and recovers rather
            than jerking — that is usually a drive-side issue rather than tension. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt that slows down when you step on it</a>
            separates the two, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping guide</a>
            covers the full symptom set.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="over-tightening" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Over-Tightening Does</h2>
<p>
            Over-tightening is the expensive mistake in this job, and it is expensive precisely
            because it feels like success. The symptom that prompted the adjustment goes away, the
            belt feels firm, and the damage happens slowly and out of sight.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bearing wear.</strong> Belt tension is a load on both roller assemblies, and
            their bearings carry it. Excess tension shortens bearing life and is a common origin of
            a rumble or squeal from the front of the machine that owners mistake for a motor fault.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">noise diagnosis guide</a>
            helps separate roller noise from the other sources.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Permanent belt stretch.</strong> A belt held under excess tension deforms and
            does not recover. That produces the machine that needs tightening every few weeks, a
            loop that ends with a replacement belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor and board heat.</strong> More tension presses the belt harder onto the
            deck, which means more friction, more current and more heat — the same thermal
            problem a dry deck causes, and the controller board is typically what fails from it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A harsher ride.</strong> A belt pulled hard against the deck transmits more of
            each footstrike, partly defeating the machine's cushioning. If a treadmill has felt
            harder underfoot since someone last adjusted it, that is a likely reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you think the belt is over-tight now, the fix is the reverse of tightening: unplug,
            back both bolts off a quarter turn anticlockwise, equally, and repeat until the lift
            test reads two to three inches, then re-check for slip and tracking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stretched-or-worn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. When Adjustment Will Not Hold</h2>
<p>
            A belt correctly set on a healthy machine keeps its tension for many months. A new
            belt stretches a little in its first few weeks and usually needs one follow-up
            adjustment, after which it settles. Anything beyond that is information.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the belt drifts again within a day or two of being centred, the rollers are rarely
            the real cause. The frame is most likely not sitting flat — check it with a spirit
            level front, rear and along a side rail, and adjust the rear feet until it does not
            rock. Carpet is a frequent culprit, because it compresses unevenly under the feet for
            weeks; our page on a
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill on carpet</a>
            explains the rigid-board fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the belt keeps going slack, unplug the machine and inspect it. Frayed or hardened
            edges mean it has been rubbing the frame. An underside that feels rough or looks
            polished means it has been running dry. Cracking across the surface, or adjuster bolts
            wound almost to the end of their travel, means the belt has reached the end of its
            life. Belts are consumables, and replacing one is routine maintenance rather than a
            failure; our guide to
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often a belt should be replaced</a>
            covers the signs and the typical intervals. Check the deck at the same time, since a new
            belt on a worn deck will not last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. How Often to Check Belt Adjustment</h2>
<p>
            There is no fixed schedule for tightening, and there should not be one — a belt that is
            correctly set does not need routine re-tensioning, and turning bolts on a timetable is
            how belts get over-tightened. What is worth doing regularly is checking.
          </p>
          <p>
            A sensible routine is to glance at tracking every time you use the machine, which
            costs nothing, and to do the lift test and the foot-plant test whenever you lubricate
            the deck, since you will have the belt lifted anyway. Adjust only when one of those
            checks fails. Re-check after moving the machine, after a new belt has bedded in for a
            couple of weeks, and after any change of flooring.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fitting these checks into a wider routine is straightforward; our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a>
            sets out what to do weekly, monthly and yearly, and none of it takes long.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. The Bottom Line on Adjusting a Treadmill Belt</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Check lubrication before you touch a bolt.</strong> A dry deck feels exactly
            like a loose belt, and tightening a belt to overcome friction loads the motor twice
            over.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tension first, with both bolts, equally.</strong> Unplugged, a quarter turn
            clockwise each side, test at about 3 mph, repeat only if it still slips. Stop when the
            slipping stops and confirm the belt still lifts two to three inches at the centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tracking second, with one bolt.</strong> Belt running empty, the side it
            drifts toward, a quarter turn clockwise, then thirty seconds of waiting before the next
            move. If the adjustment will not hold, stop turning and look at the floor, the frame and
            the belt itself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">14. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you adjust the belt on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the two bolts at the rear corners that move the rear roller. For tension, unplug the machine and turn both bolts a quarter turn clockwise, equally, then test at about 3 mph and repeat only if the belt still slips. For tracking, run the belt empty and turn the bolt on the side it drifts toward a quarter turn clockwise, then wait thirty seconds before adjusting again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you tighten a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the deck is lubricated first. Then unplug the treadmill, turn each rear roller bolt a quarter turn clockwise with the supplied hex key — exactly the same on both sides — plug in and test by planting your weight at about 3 mph. Repeat in quarter turns until the slipping stops, then confirm you can still lift the belt two to three inches at the centre of the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way do you turn the bolts to tighten a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clockwise tightens on most home treadmills, because it draws the rear roller backward; anticlockwise loosens. Turn both bolts by the same amount for tension. If your manual describes a different arrangement, follow the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tight should a treadmill belt be after adjusting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tight enough not to slip when you plant your weight at walking pace, and loose enough that you can lift it two to three inches off the deck at the centre with the machine off. Under two inches of lift is over-tight. If your manual gives a specific gap or bolt torque, use that instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill belt keep slipping after I tighten it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because tension was never the problem. A dry deck, a stretched belt, a glazed front roller or a slipping drive belt under the motor hood all produce slip that tightening does not cure. Lubricate and re-test first; if the belt slips while lifting less than two inches, stop tightening and inspect the belt and drive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you adjust treadmill belt tension or tracking first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tension first. A slack belt will not hold a tracking adjustment, and any tension change disturbs tracking slightly. Set tension with both bolts equally, then correct tracking with one bolt at a time, and re-check the lift test at the end.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Adjusting a Treadmill Belt</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a fifteen-minute job into a damaged belt or an over-loaded motor, and the first one is by far the most common.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening a belt that actually needs lubricating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dry deck makes the belt hesitate under load exactly as a slack one does. Tightening stops the symptom by giving the roller more grip, but adds a tension load on top of the friction load, and the motor and controller board carry both from then on. Feel the deck under the belt before you pick up the hex key.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning the bolts unequally</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Tension adjustments must be identical on both sides. A quarter turn on one side and a half on the other steers the belt, so you finish with the slip fixed and a tracking problem added. Count and write down every quarter turn on each side as you go.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening until it feels firm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Nothing underfoot tells you when a belt has gone past correct, so tightening by feel has no upper limit. The lift test is the limit: if you cannot raise the belt two inches at the centre of the deck, it is over-tight, and the bearings, belt and motor will pay for it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Making tracking turns faster than the belt responds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A quarter turn changes the direction the belt travels, not its position, and the migration takes around twenty to forty seconds at 3 mph. Turning again in that window stacks adjustments, and the belt ends up against the opposite rail. One quarter turn, wait thirty seconds, then decide.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

